<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al Hoceima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alhucemas, Villa Sanjurjo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al Hoceima is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, in the Rif. It lies near the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekor">Nekor</a>, capital of an early Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>. The modern town was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as Villa Sanjurjo after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhucemas-landing">Alhucemas landing</a> of 1925 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> and returned to Morocco in 1956. In 2016–2017 it was the centre of the Hirak Rif protest movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hoceima.jpg" alt="Al Hoceima" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdel Jalil Bouhaj · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Hoceima_Quemado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Al Hoceima lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif">Rif Mountains</a>, in the heart of the Central Rif, surrounded by the range's highest peaks, among them Mount Tidghin at 2,452 metres. It is the capital of Al Hoceima Province and remains a cultural centre for the Ayt Waryaghar tribe. Its name is an Arabised form of Alhucemas, the Spanish name of the nearby Spanish-owned Alhucemas Islands, meaning lavender islands, itself from the Andalusi Arabic al-Khuzāmā. Some, among them the author of the Spanish encyclopedia Espasa-Calpe, derive Alhucemas from al-Mazimma. The climate is a hot-summer Mediterranean one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ait Ouriaghel Berber tribe, also called Beni Urriaguel, dominated the area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-el-krim">Abd el-Krim</a>, whose father was a qadi of the Ait Youssef ou Ali clan, organised a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> force against the Spanish during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> and established the Republic of the Rif in 1921, which lasted until 1926. In September 1925 the Spanish general José Sanjurjo landed at Al Hoceima and claimed the territory for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Spanish developed the town and named it Villa Sanjurjo; its first mayor was Florian Gómez Aroca. The beach where Sanjurjo and his troops landed, Playa Quemado, is still one of its well-known beaches.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> became independent in 1956 the town grew quickly and the government gave it the name Al Hoceima, following the standard French spelling. The years from 1956 to 1959 were hard in the Rif. In October 1958 the Beni Urriaguel rebelled against the central administration, and two thirds of the Moroccan army, led by the crown prince, the future Hassan II, were sent to Al Hoceima; a significant number of people lost their lives in the Rif in this period.</p><p>Two earthquakes struck the city and its villages within ten years: one of magnitude 6.0 on 26 May 1994 and one of magnitude 6.4 on 24 February 2004, which killed more than 560 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Al Hoceima is a moderate-size city whose income comes from fishing and tourism, and it has the second-largest port of the Rif region after Nador. Many of its former inhabitants migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the 1960s and the 1980s, and large numbers of Moroccans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> come from the city; many return in summer. The first schools built by the Spanish, a college and an elementary school, and a Spanish Catholic church still exist. In the early 1950s and 1960s the small houses were mostly painted white and blue, colours that were regarded as the city's own, and in 2007 the mayor stated that new houses would be painted in them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al Hoceima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His descendants, the Salihids, ruled the emirate, and his grandson Said ibn Idris built the capital Nekor near modern <b>Al Hoceima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nekor">Emirate of Nekor</a></li></ul></section>
